---
import ToolLayout from "@site/layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { CSP } from "../web/csp.ts";
// Library versions and licences are read from the installed packages when the site is built, so this list can't go stale.
const lib = (name: string) => {
  const p = JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(process.cwd(), "node_modules", name, "package.json"), "utf8"));
  return { name, version: p.version as string, licence: p.license as string };
};
const libs = [
  { ...lib("pdfjs-dist"), what: "PDF.js, Mozilla's PDF reader (the one inside Firefox). Shows the page previews, draws pages as images and reads their text." },
  { ...lib("@cantoo/pdf-lib"), what: "pdf-lib (a maintained fork). Writes the new PDFs: copies, reorders, rotates and resizes pages, and places images on pages." },
  { ...lib("fflate"), what: "Packs several output files into one .zip file." },
  { ...lib("utif2"), what: "Reads and writes TIFF images." },
];
const repo = "https://github.com/mehfoos/halfbakedtools/blob/main/tools/pdf-tools";
---
<ToolLayout slug="pdf-tools" page="methods">
  <h1>Methods and limits</h1>

  <h2>Your files are not uploaded</h2>
  <p>Most online PDF tools send your file to a company's computer, change it there and send it back. This one doesn't. When you choose a file, your browser reads it into the page's memory, the code on this page changes it there, and the result is handed back to you as a download made inside the page. Closing or reloading the tab discards everything.</p>
  <p>You don't have to take that on trust. Three things back it up:</p>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>There is nowhere to upload to.</strong> This site is a set of fixed files served by GitHub Pages. It has no server program that could receive or store a file.</li>
    <li><strong>The browser is told to block it.</strong> The tool page carries a security policy (a Content-Security-Policy) that makes your browser refuse any connection from the page to any other site, and any form submission. The libraries listed below are bound by it too, including the part of the PDF reader that runs in the background (a "worker"): that part is started from code held inside the page, because browsers do not apply a page's policy to a worker started from a web address. The policy is: <code>{CSP}</code></li>
    <li><strong>The code is open and tested for it.</strong> All of the tool's <a href={`${repo}/web`}>source code</a> is public, and an <a href={`${repo}/web/privacy.test.ts`}>automated check</a> fails, and stops the site being published, if the tool's own code ever contains a way of sending data over the network or loses the policy above.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To check for yourself: open your browser's developer tools (F12), choose the Network tab, then use the tool. You will see the page's own files being fetched from this site, and nothing being sent. Or load the page, add a file to check the previews work, switch off your internet connection, and carry on. One caveat for working offline: a few parts are fetched from this site only when first needed (TIFF support, and extra fonts and decoders for some unusual PDFs), so those can fail offline if you haven't used them yet.</p>
  <p>Two honest limits to this promise. It covers this page, not your device: browser extensions and other software on your computer can see what you open, as with any website. And like any website, GitHub, which hosts the site, sees that your browser requested the page (your internet address and the time), though never your files. See the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}privacy/`}>privacy page</a>.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Arranging, merging, splitting and extracting.</strong> Pages are copied into a new PDF exactly as they are. Text stays as text, and pictures are not compressed again, so nothing loses quality. Only the order, rotation and size of pages change.</li>
    <li><strong>Turning pages.</strong> A turn is recorded as the page's rotation setting, added to any rotation the file already had. The page's contents are not redrawn.</li>
    <li><strong>Resizing.</strong> The paper size and everything on the page are scaled by the same percentage, so the page looks the same, only larger or smaller.</li>
    <li><strong>Editing (adding text and pictures).</strong> Added text is written into the page as real text, in one of the PDF format's built-in fonts, so it stays sharp, selectable and searchable. Added pictures are placed as they are. Additions are drawn on top of the page's existing contents, which are not changed. The editor shows each page rebuilt exactly as it will be saved, so what you see is what you get. Additions belong to their page: they turn, resize, duplicate and move with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Page numbers.</strong> Each number is written as real text on top of the page, placed by the corner and margin you choose on the page as it is finally seen, so sideways and resized pages are numbered in the right place and at the same size. Numbers count within each saved file. "Start on page" leaves earlier pages unnumbered, and totals ("of 5") count numbered pages only.</li>
    <li><strong>Shapes, highlight and drawing.</strong> Rectangles, ellipses, lines, highlights and freehand lines are drawn on top of the page. A highlight is a see-through coloured box that you place by eye; it does not know where the text is and does not change the text. Like all additions here, they cover or mark what is on the page without removing it.</li>
    <li><strong>Sign.</strong> A signature you draw is placed on the page as a picture of your handwriting (with a see-through background). It is <em>not</em> a digital or cryptographic signature: it cannot prove who signed or that the document is unchanged, and anyone who has the PDF can copy the picture. The drawing exists only on the page while you work; it is not stored or remembered.</li>
    <li><strong>Crop.</strong> Cutting margins sets the page's visible area (its "crop box"). Everything outside it is hidden when the PDF is opened or printed by ordinary software, but it is still stored in the file, and some programs can show it again. The same margins, in millimetres, inches or points, are used on every page you choose, whatever its size. Pages already cropped by other software are measured from what they currently show.</li>
    <li><strong>Watermark.</strong> The words are written on top of every page as see-through text, once in the middle or repeated in staggered rows, at the angle and strength you choose. Like page numbers, they are placed on each page as it is finally seen.</li>
    <li><strong>Protecting with a password.</strong> The saved PDF is encrypted (AES with a 256-bit key, the strongest method the PDF library offers), so its contents can't be read without the password. One password is set; whoever has it can open, print, copy and change the document. The password is used in your browser to lock the file and is not kept anywhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Unlocking.</strong> If you know a PDF's password, the tool opens it and saves a copy that needs no password. It does not guess or break passwords. Locks an author has set against copying or changing, on a PDF that opens without a password, are not carried into saved copies either.</li>
    <li><strong>Compressing.</strong> Most of a large PDF's size is usually its pictures. The tool finds the pictures stored in the file, shrinks any that hold more detail than the setting allows, and stores them again as JPEG at the chosen quality. A picture is only replaced if the new copy is smaller. Text, fonts and line drawings are not touched, so text stays sharp and selectable, and page sizes do not change. This is also how to make a scanned PDF's pictures smaller without changing its paper size. The new size shown is the real size of the finished file, not a prediction. The Extreme level keeps text but makes pictures visibly rough. The Maximum level, and the custom "Turn whole pages into pictures" method, redraw each page as a single JPEG, which loses the text; "Tidy up only" repacks the file without touching any content.</li>
    <li><strong>Pages to images.</strong> Each page is drawn at the detail you choose (dots per inch) and saved as a picture. PNG, TIFF and BMP keep every pixel; JPEG and WebP make smaller files by discarding fine detail, more so at lower quality.</li>
    <li><strong>Pages to text.</strong> The words are read out of the PDF in the order the file stores them.</li>
    <li><strong>Images to PDF.</strong> Each image becomes one page, sized from its pixels and the dots-per-inch setting. JPEG files are placed in the PDF unchanged. PNG files keep every pixel. Other formats are converted to PNG first, which also keeps every pixel. A phone photo's orientation note is respected, so photos come out the right way up.</li>
    <li><strong>File names.</strong> Saved files get a name that differs from your original's (for example <code>report-merged.pdf</code>), so the original isn't replaced by accident. If you type a name that matches a file you added, the tool stops and asks before saving. It can only compare against the files you added: a web page cannot look inside your folders, so it cannot know what else is there. Whether your browser then warns before replacing a file is up to the browser.</li>
    <li><strong>The settings file.</strong> It lists each file you added (name, size and a SHA-256 checksum, which is a fingerprint of the file's contents) and every page of your arrangement (which file and page it came from, its rotation and its size), plus your save options. It contains no document content and no passwords. When you load it and add the files again, they are matched by fingerprint, so a renamed copy still matches and a changed file doesn't.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Software used</h2>
  <p>These open-source libraries are bundled into the site and served from it; nothing is loaded from another site.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Library</th><th>Version</th><th>Licence</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{libs.map((l) => <tr><td><code>{l.name}</code></td><td>{l.version}</td><td>{l.licence}</td><td>{l.what}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What has been checked</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Checked by automated tests</strong> on every change: page-range typing, every page edit, the split plans, the settings file (including rejecting damaged files), and the written PDFs (page order, repeated pages, rotation, resizing, images placed as pages), the rules for compression (how detail is estimated, which pictures are eligible), and where added text and pictures are placed on pages of every rotation. See the <a href={`${repo}/VERIFICATION.md`}>verification log</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Run end to end in one browser so far</strong> (Chrome on a computer, 16 November 2026, with small test files): every feature above, including password protection (the result checked with two other programs), compression of generated scan-like files and adding text and pictures to plain, sideways, cropped and turned pages, and that the browser blocked a deliberate attempt to contact another site. Firefox, Safari and phones are <strong>not yet checked</strong>, and nor are large or complicated real-world PDFs.</li>
    <li><strong>Checked against the PDF standard:</strong> the tool relies on one fixed number, that a PDF's unit of length is 1/72 of an inch unless the file says otherwise. This was checked on 16 November 2026 against the PDF standard (PDF 32000-1:2008, the edition Adobe publishes of ISO 32000-1; section 8.3.2.3 on page 115, and the UserUnit entry of Table 30 on page 79). The copy used is not yet in this site's archive of sources, so it is not yet listed as a numbered reference.</li>
    <li><strong>Checked in Chrome, 16 November 2026:</strong> a background worker started from a web address could contact another site despite the page's policy; one started from code held in the page could not. The tool starts its only worker the second way, and a test fails if that changes. Other browsers are <strong>not yet checked</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Long page lists are shown a screenful at a time.</strong> The number of pages per screen follows the size of your screen (or your choice). "Select all" and "Select none" cover the whole document, not just the pages in view, and the line above the list says how many are selected. You can drag a page to a new place only within the screen you are on; to move it further, use "Move earlier", "Move later" or "Move selected to position", which take you to the page's new screen.</li>
    <li><strong>Cropping is not removal.</strong> Anything cut off a page stays in the saved file and can be recovered by someone with the right software. Do not use Crop to hide private information. A tool that truly removes content is planned.</li>
    <li><strong>Editing only adds.</strong> It cannot yet change or remove text or pictures already on a page. The tool deliberately offers no "cover it with a box" option: a covered word is still in the file and can be copied out, so it would look like removal without being it.</li>
    <li><strong>Added text uses Western European characters only</strong> (letters with common accents, digits, punctuation, £ and €). Other scripts, and symbols such as Ω or emoji, can't be written yet; the editor says which characters are the problem and won't save until they are removed. Three typefaces are offered, each in regular and bold.</li>
    <li><strong>A watermark is a label, not protection.</strong> It is ordinary text added to the page, and anyone with PDF software can take it out again. A faint grey watermark can also be hard to see over photographs or dark areas: check the preview. Picture watermarks (a logo) are not built yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Page numbers don't know what is already on the page.</strong> If a page already has a number printed, or other content where the number goes, the new number is drawn over it. Check the preview and move the position if needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Small previews don't show additions.</strong> The page list marks pages that have them; open the page to see them.</li>
    <li><strong>Not built yet:</strong> changing or removing existing text and pictures within a page; converting to and from Word, PowerPoint, Excel and web pages; text files to PDF; saving pages as SVG drawings. These are planned; see the <a href={`${repo}/ROADMAP.md`}>roadmap</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Compression is lossy and can't be undone.</strong> Shrunk pictures lose fine detail for good, so keep your original. Small print inside a scanned page can become hard to read at strong settings: use the before-and-after preview, which shows the first page only.</li>
    <li><strong>How picture detail is judged.</strong> The tool estimates a picture's dots per inch as if it filled its page. A small picture on a large page really has more dots per inch than the estimate, so it may be shrunk less than the setting suggests, never more.</li>
    <li><strong>Pictures the compressor leaves alone.</strong> Black-and-white fax-style scans, pictures in print (CMYK) or palette colours, pictures with special masks, and some less common storage formats are not changed, and the result panel says how many were skipped and why. Pictures with a colour profile are stored without it afterwards, which can shift colours slightly. Grey pictures are stored as colour JPEGs.</li>
    <li><strong>Turning whole pages into pictures</strong> removes selectable text, links and form fields, and makes the document unreadable to screen readers.</li>
    <li><strong>Things a new PDF doesn't keep.</strong> Saved PDFs are made of copied pages. Parts of a PDF that belong to the whole document rather than to a page may be lost or stop working: bookmarks (the outline), fillable form fields, attached files, and the document's title and author. Links that jump to another page may stop working.</li>
    <li><strong>Digital signatures</strong> are invalidated by any change, as they are meant to be.</li>
    <li><strong>Password-protected PDFs</strong> can be opened if you know the password, but the PDFs you save from the other tools are not password-protected; use Protect PDF afterwards if you need that. The same goes for PDFs their author has locked against changes or copying: saved copies don't carry the lock. Only work on files you have the right to change.</li>
    <li><strong>A lost password can't be recovered</strong> by this tool or by this site, which never sees it. A short or guessable password can be found by trying many: the protection is only as good as the password. Very old PDF viewers may not open files protected this way.</li>
    <li><strong>Removing a page is not redaction of a page's contents.</strong> A removed page is truly absent from the saved PDF, but this tool can't yet remove words or pictures from within a page.</li>
    <li><strong>Scans have no text.</strong> A scanned page is a photograph of text. This tool does not do text recognition (OCR), so saving such pages as text gives nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Text order and layout.</strong> Saved text has no columns, tables or formatting, and can come out in an odd order for pages with complex layouts.</li>
    <li><strong>Images.</strong> Only the first image in a multi-image TIFF is used. An SVG drawing is placed as a picture at its stated size, not as a scalable drawing. Animated images use one frame. Saved TIFF and BMP files are uncompressed and large.</li>
    <li><strong>Large files.</strong> Everything is held in your device's memory. Very large PDFs, or saving many pages as high-detail images, can be slow or fail, especially on phones.</li>
    <li><strong>Unusual or damaged PDFs</strong> may fail to open, or show a preview that differs slightly from other PDF viewers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Found a file that comes out wrong? Please <a href="https://github.com/mehfoos/halfbakedtools/issues">report it</a>, without attaching anything private.</p>
</ToolLayout>
